{"count": 20437, "next": "https://api.acmi.net.au/creators/?page=893", "previous": "https://api.acmi.net.au/creators/?page=891", "results": [{"id": 76481, "name": "Leslie Howard", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2233639, "title": "Pygmalion", "work_id": 79535, "role": "co-director", "role_id": 959, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 1990087, "title": "Intermezzo", "work_id": 85624, "role": "cast", "role_id": 33, "is_primary": false}, {"id": 902071, "title": "Of human bondage. Rain", "work_id": 82812, "role": "cast", "role_id": 33, "is_primary": false}], "also_known_as": "Leslie Howard Stainer", "date_of_birth": "1893-04-03", "date_of_death": "1943-06-01", "places_of_operation": [{"id": 10, "name": "United Kingdom", "slug": "united-kingdom"}], "biography": "Leslie Howard Steiner (3 April 1893 – 1 June 1943) was an English actor, director, producer and writer. He wrote many stories and articles for The New York Times, The New Yorker, and Vanity Fair and was one of the biggest box-office draws and movie idols of the 1930s.\nActive in both Britain and Hollywood, Howard played  Ashley Wilkes in Gone with the Wind (1939). He had roles in many other films, often playing the quintessential Englishman, including Berkeley Square (1933), Of Human Bondage (1934), The Scarlet Pimpernel (1934), The Petrified Forest (1936), Pygmalion (1938), Intermezzo (1939), \"Pimpernel\" Smith (1941), and The First of the Few (1942). He was nominated for the Academy Award for Best Actor for Berkeley Square and Pygmalion.\nHoward's World War II activities included acting and filmmaking. He helped to make anti-German propaganda and shore up support for the Allies—two years after his death the British Film Yearbook described Howard's work as \"one of the most valuable facets of British propaganda\". He was rumoured to have been involved with British or Allied Intelligence, sparking conspiracy theories regarding his death in 1943 when the Luftwaffe shot down BOAC Flight 777 over the Atlantic (off the coast of Cedeira, A Coruña), on which he was a passenger.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-08-01T15:55:52.874672+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 2574, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leslie_Howard"}], "uuid": "286f26b4-7e3b-4cac-9ba9-278a3065a62f", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "23775", "external_references": [{"id": 10996, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q156178"}, {"id": 10997, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "22278306"}, {"id": 10998, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n81095890"}, {"id": 10999, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n81095890"}], "date_modified": "2023-08-01T15:55:52.886337+10:00"}, {"id": 76480, "name": "Jack Conway", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2322167, "title": "Tarzan and his mate", "work_id": 95635, "role": "co-director", "role_id": 959, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 2075892, "title": "A Tale of Two Cities", "work_id": 79532, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": "1887-07-17", "date_of_death": "1952-10-11", "places_of_operation": [{"id": 1, "name": "United States of America", "slug": "united-states-of-america"}], "biography": "Hugh Ryan \"Jack\" Conway (July 17, 1886 – October 11, 1952) was an American film director and film producer, as well as an actor of many films in the first half of the 20th century.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-08-01T15:17:04.018203+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 2278, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Conway_(filmmaker)"}], "uuid": "e0908fb9-3e6d-4591-908c-c560fd0d847b", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "4398", "external_references": [{"id": 9785, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q141673"}, {"id": 9786, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "19887002"}, {"id": 9787, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n86041388"}, {"id": 9788, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n86041388"}], "date_modified": "2023-08-01T15:17:04.037207+10:00"}, {"id": 76479, "name": "Greer Garson", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2228975, "title": "Pride and prejudice", "work_id": 79529, "role": "cast", "role_id": 33, "is_primary": false}], "also_known_as": "Eileen Evelyn Greer Garson", "date_of_birth": "1904-09-29", "date_of_death": "1996-04-06", "places_of_operation": [{"id": 10, "name": "United Kingdom", "slug": "united-kingdom"}], "biography": "Eileen Evelyn Greer Garson  (29 September 1904 – 6 April 1996) was a British-American actress and singer. She was a major star at Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer who became popular during the Second World War for her portrayal of strong women on the homefront; listed by the Motion Picture Herald as one of America's top-ten box office draws from 1942 to 1946.\nThe fourth most-nominated woman for the Best Actress Oscar, Garson received seven Academy Award nominations, including a record-tying (with Bette Davis) five consecutive nominations (1941–1945) in the actress category, winning for her performance in the title role of the 1942 film Mrs. Miniver.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-08-01T15:04:36.017034+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 4100, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greer_Garson"}], "uuid": "41af71ca-2849-4fd7-b248-ed50e884de06", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "33500", "external_references": [{"id": 25149, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q182486"}, {"id": 28755, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "2733115"}, {"id": 28756, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n85151613"}, {"id": 28757, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n85151613"}], "date_modified": "2023-08-01T15:04:40.489563+10:00"}, {"id": 76478, "name": "Cedric Gibbons", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2322165, "title": "Tarzan and his mate", "work_id": 95635, "role": "co-director", "role_id": 959, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 2228971, "title": "Pride and prejudice", "work_id": 79529, "role": "designer", "role_id": 495, "is_primary": false}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": "1893-03-23", "date_of_death": "1960-07-26", "places_of_operation": [{"id": 1, "name": "United States of America", "slug": "united-states-of-america"}], "biography": "Austin Cedric Gibbons (March 23, 1890 – July 26, 1960) was an Irish-American art director for the film industry. He also made a significant contribution to motion picture theater architecture from the 1930s to 1950s. Gibbons designed the Oscar statuette in 1928, but tasked the sculpting to George Stanley, a Los Angeles artist. He was nominated 39 times for the Academy Award for Best Production Design and won the Oscar 11 times, both of which are records.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-08-01T14:21:59.824488+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 1786, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cedric_Gibbons"}], "uuid": "15acec09-4110-4488-ad0e-bb112ccece4e", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "4397", "external_references": [{"id": 7780, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q727904"}, {"id": 7781, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "42042359"}, {"id": 7782, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n85342791"}, {"id": 7783, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n85342791"}], "date_modified": "2023-08-01T14:21:59.836494+10:00"}, {"id": 76477, "name": "Robert J Kern", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2228969, "title": "Pride and prejudice", "work_id": 79529, "role": "editor", "role_id": 918, "is_primary": true}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": "", "date_of_death": "", "places_of_operation": [], "biography": "", "biography_author": null, "date_of_biography": "2023-05-09T16:31:22.914259+10:00", "external_links": [], "uuid": "0b253b74-9980-440d-8e08-98b936012e34", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "38223", "external_references": [], "date_modified": "2023-06-14T16:33:30.576250+10:00"}, {"id": 76476, "name": "Aldous Huxley", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2228966, "title": "Pride and prejudice", "work_id": 79529, "role": "screenwriter", "role_id": 601, "is_primary": false}], "also_known_as": "Aldous Leonard Huxley", "date_of_birth": "1894-07-26", "date_of_death": "1963-11-22", "places_of_operation": [{"id": 10, "name": "United Kingdom", "slug": "united-kingdom"}], "biography": "Aldous Leonard Huxley ( AWL-dəs; 26 July 1894 – 22 November 1963) was an English writer and philosopher. His bibliography spans nearly 50 books, including novels and non-fiction works, as well as essays, narratives, and poems.\nBorn into the prominent Huxley family, he graduated from Balliol College, Oxford, with an undergraduate degree in English literature. Early in his career, he published short stories and poetry and edited the literary magazine Oxford Poetry, before going on to publish travel writing, satire, and screenplays. He spent the latter part of his life in the United States, living in Los Angeles from 1937 until his death. By the end of his life, Huxley was widely acknowledged as one of the foremost intellectuals of his time. He was nominated for the Nobel Prize in Literature nine times, and was elected Companion of Literature by the Royal Society of Literature in 1962.Huxley was a pacifist. He grew interested in philosophical mysticism, as well as universalism, addressing these subjects in his works such as The Perennial Philosophy, which illustrates commonalities between Western and Eastern mysticism, and The Doors of Perception, which interprets his own psychedelic experience with mescaline. In his most famous novel Brave New World and his final novel Island, he presented his visions of dystopia and utopia, respectively.\n\n", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-08-01T13:57:27.469695+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 3515, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldous_Huxley"}], "uuid": "1ad4296e-6270-4c29-9504-7d489ff28977", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "272", "external_references": [{"id": 25088, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q81447"}, {"id": 27160, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "71392434"}, {"id": 27161, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n80057246"}, {"id": 27162, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n80057246"}], "date_modified": "2023-08-01T13:57:31.418908+10:00"}, {"id": 76475, "name": "Robert Z Leonard", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2228983, "title": "Pride and prejudice (S) (Captioned)", "work_id": 85193, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 2228964, "title": "Pride and prejudice", "work_id": 79529, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 2212211, "title": "Duchess of Idaho", "work_id": 95618, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 2171336, "title": "Maytime", "work_id": 81463, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 1978849, "title": "Nancy goes to Rio", "work_id": 95566, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}, {"id": 786354, "title": "Girl of the Golden West", "work_id": 84596, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}], "also_known_as": "Robert Zigler Leonard", "date_of_birth": "1889-10-07", "date_of_death": "1968-08-27", "places_of_operation": [{"id": 1, "name": "United States of America", "slug": "united-